CAT = "mtguardLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
# Private Guards and Community Officers Act, Cap. 389 (formerly the Private Guards and Local Wardens
# Act), arts. 2 to 13, 21 to 25; Private Guards Regulations, S.L. 389.01, regs. 3 to 12. Read on
# legislation.mt, November 2026. No fees, fines or money limits.
Q = [
("In Malta, who issues a private guard licence?",
 "The Commissioner of Police", ["The Minister for Home Affairs", "Jobsplus, after the course", "The Malta Police Association"],
 "Article 6 of the Private Guards and Community Officers Act (Cap. 389) says an application to be licensed as a private guard is made in writing to the Commissioner, defined in article 2 as the Commissioner of Police. Jobsplus runs a training course but does not license anyone."),
("Cap. 389, the law on private guards, is now called the Private Guards and:",
 "Community Officers Act", ["Local Enforcement Act", "Security Services Act", "Private Security Services Act"],
 "Act XIV of 2018 renamed the Private Guards and Local Wardens Act as the Private Guards and Community Officers Act. Local wardens are now called community officers."),
("A private guard agency wants to place a new recruit on a site before his licence comes through. What does the Act say?",
 "It may not provide him", ["It may, for one month", "It may, if he is supervised", "It may, at night only"],
 "Article 5 of Cap. 389 says no private guard agency shall provide any person for duties in private guard services unless that person is licensed as a private guard."),
("A shop owner hires an unlicensed friend to guard his warehouse for a fee. Who breaks article 4 of Cap. 389?",
 "The shop owner who hires him", ["Nobody, it is a private deal", "Only the warehouse insurer", "Only the friend's family"],
 "Article 4 forbids anyone to employ, engage or permit the use of private guard services unless they come from a licensed agency or licensed guards. The friend also breaks article 3 by acting as a guard unlicensed."),
("Which of these is a private guard service under article 2 of Cap. 389?",
 "Protecting people from harm", ["Issuing parking tickets", "Enforcing litter bye-laws", "Directing traffic on a road"],
 "The definition lists the prevention of intrusion and trespass on private property, the protection of individuals from bodily harm, the protection of property and cash in transit, among others. Bye-law enforcement is the work of community officers."),
("Who enforces local council bye-laws, such as litter rules, under Cap. 389?",
 "Community officers", ["Private guards", "Specialised private guards", "Guards at places of entertainment"],
 "Part III of Cap. 389 covers community officers, whose services are those local councils need to control, regulate and enforce bye-laws. A private guard has no power to enforce them."),
("Which category of private guard licence may guard money in transit?",
 "Specialised private guard (driving)", ["Private guard", "Private guard at a place of entertainment", "Specialised private guard (non-driving)"],
 "Regulation 7(4) of the Private Guards Regulations (S.L. 389.01) says only a specialised private guard (driving) may provide services for the transportation or guarding in transit of property or money."),
("How many specialised private guards must at least crew a vehicle carrying cash in transit?",
 "Two", ["One", "Three", "Four"],
 "Regulation 12(2) of S.L. 389.01 says a vehicle transporting or guarding money or property in transit shall be manned by at least two specialised private guards, and regulation 12(1) requires armoured vehicles."),
("What experience does an applicant need for a licence to guard a place of entertainment?",
 "Five years in a listed service", ["One year as a private guard", "Two years at any bar or club", "Three years in any customer job"],
 "Regulation 4(2) of S.L. 389.01 asks for five years with the police, the armed forces or the prison services, in Malta or abroad, or at least five years in total as a private guard, with references."),
("Who must approve a training course that qualifies people for a private guard licence?",
 "The Commissioner of Police", ["The Malta Chamber of Commerce", "Any licensed guard agency", "The Ministry for Tourism"],
 "Article 23 of Cap. 389 says no one may provide courses for the qualifications needed for a private guard licence unless the Commissioner has approved them. Regulation 5 of S.L. 389.01 sets out how a provider applies."),
("To renew his licence, what training must a private guard show for the previous year?",
 "At least ten hours in all", ["At least forty hours", "A full new basic course", "None, renewal is automatic"],
 "Regulation 4(3) of S.L. 389.01 says a guard does not qualify for renewal unless he proves he attended a continuing training course or courses of not less than ten hours in the aggregate during the previous year."),
("How long is a private guard licence valid from its date of issue under article 12 of Cap. 389?",
 "One year", ["Six months", "Three years", "Five years"],
 "Article 12(1)(a) says a licence is valid for one year from its date of issue, and article 12(2) lets it be renewed from year to year."),
("A licensed guard moves house. What does his licence require him to do?",
 "Inform the Commissioner", ["Wait until his renewal", "Tell his client only", "Inform the local council"],
 "Article 12(1)(c) of Cap. 389 makes every licence subject to the condition that the holder informs the Commissioner of any change in the information given in the application."),
("Does a private guard licence give the holder extra rights over other people's property?",
 "No, none it did not have before", ["Yes, the right to enter any house", "Yes, police powers on duty", "Yes, on the client's premises only"],
 "Article 12(3) of Cap. 389 says a licence does not give the holder any right or privilege in relation to third parties or their property that he did not have before the Act."),
("Who owns the identity card the Commissioner issues to a licensed private guard?",
 "The Commissioner", ["The guard", "The guard's agency", "The client on site"],
 "Regulation 10(1) of S.L. 389.01 says the identity card remains the property of the Commissioner, and regulation 10(3) says it is returned when it expires."),
("On duty, where must a private guard wear his identity card?",
 "In a conspicuous place", ["Inside his wallet", "Inside a jacket pocket", "At the guard room desk"],
 "Regulation 10(4) of S.L. 389.01 says every licensed private guard shall wear the identity card in a conspicuous place while on duty, unless exempted with the uniform."),
("What happens to a guard's identity card if his licence is withdrawn?",
 "It counts as expired", ["It stays valid to its date", "It becomes a visitor pass", "It passes to the agency"],
 "Regulation 10(2) of S.L. 389.01 says that if the licence is suspended, withdrawn or not renewed, the card is deemed to have expired on that event, and it must be returned to the Commissioner."),
("Which guards are not required to wear a uniform approved by the Commissioner?",
 "Guards at entertainment venues", ["Guards on night shifts", "Guards at hospital sites", "Guards at large shopping centres"],
 "Regulation 9(3) of S.L. 389.01 says private guards rendering services at a place of entertainment shall not be required to wear a uniform approved by the Commissioner. Other guards need written exemption."),
("A guard has written exemption to work out of uniform. What must he carry on duty?",
 "The exemption and his ID card", ["Only his driving licence", "A letter from the client", "A copy of his agency's contract"],
 "Regulation 9(2) of S.L. 389.01 makes the exemption subject to the guard carrying, and showing on demand, the written authorisation and the identity card issued by the Commissioner."),
("A guard does not keep to a condition of his licence. What may the Commissioner do under article 13?",
 "Withdraw or suspend it", ["Only issue a warning", "Fine the guard's client", "Wait until it expires"],
 "Article 13(1) of Cap. 389 lets the Commissioner withdraw or suspend a licence where the holder does not conform to a term or condition, or where facts arise that would have justified refusing it."),
("Where may an applicant go to challenge the refusal of a private guard licence?",
 "Administrative Review Tribunal", ["The Small Claims Tribunal", "The Industrial Tribunal", "The Court of Magistrates (Malta)"],
 "Article 11(3) of Cap. 389 lets the applicant apply to the Administrative Review Tribunal, under the Administrative Justice Act, within one calendar month of receiving the refusal."),
("Which of these is a ground on which the Commissioner must refuse a guard licence?",
 "Police discharge for misconduct", ["Living outside the harbour area", "Having no driving licence", "Holding a second part-time job"],
 "Article 10(a)(iii) of Cap. 389 lists a person discharged from the Police, the armed forces or the prison services because of an offence or other misbehaviour."),
("Why can a serving police officer not also hold a private guard licence?",
 "Article 10 rules it out", ["Police shifts are too long", "He would need a second course", "It is allowed with a permit"],
 "Article 10(a)(iv) of Cap. 389 says the Commissioner shall refuse an application from a member of the Police, the armed forces or the prison services, or a public officer outside a prescribed grade."),
("Who must hold the indemnity insurance that covers a guard employed by an agency?",
 "The employer", ["The guard himself", "The client", "The Commissioner"],
 "Article 21(1) of Cap. 389 requires every licensed person to be covered by indemnity insurance against liability for loss or damage, and says the insurance shall be made by the employer if the licensed person is an employee."),
]
